      "schoolName": "TRƯỜNG THCS ĐỒNG YÊN",
      "teacherName": "Thầy Đinh Văn Thành",
      "audioUrl": "/web-tieng-anh/audio/listening_6_gk1.mp3",
      "audioScript": "Host: Today we have Huy, the star athlete of Class 6A.\nHuy: Good morning, everyone! I'm happy to be here.\nHost: You are known for being very sporty. Which sports do you play regularly?\nHuy: I play football three times a week and swim every Sunday morning. Playing sports helps me stay healthy and energetic.\nHost: Who is your best sports companion?\nHuy: My classmate Nam. He is fast, funny, and always encourages the team during matches.\nHost: What are your plans for next weekend?\nHuy: We are having a friendly football match against Class 6B on Saturday afternoon. We hope to win!",
      "clozeText": "Duy is my closest classmate in grade 6. He is tall and has short black hair with a (23) ________ face. He is very (24) ________ because he always gets good marks in maths and science. Besides studying, Duy is also very (25) ________ and plays basketball every afternoon. He has a great sense of humour and tells (26) ________ stories that make everyone laugh. I am lucky to have such a (27) ________ friend.",
      "readingText": "The Tay ethnic minority people in Ha Giang usually build their homes on high wooden stilts. This unique style of architecture has existed for centuries.\n\nA typical stilt house has two levels. The upper level is the living space for the whole family, while the ground level underneath is used for storing farming tools, firewood, and weaving looms. The framework of the house is made of solid ironwood, and the steep roof is covered with palm leaves to drain heavy rainwater quickly.\n\nInside the house, there is a central hearth that never goes out. It keeps the house warm during cold mountain winters and serves as a gathering spot where elders tell folk tales to children. Visiting a Tay stilt house allows travellers to experience the authentic warmth and hospitality of highlanders.",
      "passageText": "📖 ĐOẠN VĂN ĐIỀN TỪ (CLOZE TEXT):\nDuy is my closest classmate in grade 6. He is tall and has short black hair with a (23) ________ face. He is very (24) ________ because he always gets good marks in maths and science. Besides studying, Duy is also very (25) ________ and plays basketball every afternoon. He has a great sense of humour and tells (26) ________ stories that make everyone laugh. I am lucky to have such a (27) ________ friend.\n\n📖 ĐOẠN VĂN ĐỌC HIỂU (READING PASSAGE):\nThe Tay ethnic minority people in Ha Giang usually build their homes on high wooden stilts. This unique style of architecture has existed for centuries.\n\nA typical stilt house has two levels. The upper level is the living space for the whole family, while the ground level underneath is used for storing farming tools, firewood, and weaving looms. The framework of the house is made of solid ironwood, and the steep roof is covered with palm leaves to drain heavy rainwater quickly.\n\nInside the house, there is a central hearth that never goes out. It keeps the house warm during cold mountain winters and serves as a gathering spot where elders tell folk tales to children. Visiting a Tay stilt house allows travellers to experience the authentic warmth and hospitality of highlanders.",
      "writingRubric": "Tiêu chí chấm điểm (1.0đ): You should write about: His/her name and age; Appearance (hair, eyes, face); Personality traits (kind, friendly, sporty, clever); Activities you often do together.",
      "sampleWritingText": "In my opinion, engaging in productive activities during free time plays a crucial role in teenagers' overall development. First, it provides a healthy balance between rigorous academic studies and physical recreation, helping students alleviate stress. Furthermore, participating in school clubs or community projects broadens practical knowledge and strengthens social bonding. Therefore, choosing meaningful hobbies is essential for a joyful and well-rounded teenage life.",
