      "audioScript": "In art class today, Mr. Quang asked grade 6 students to draw their concept of a dream home. Nam sketched a high-tech eco-villa powered by solar energy with a rooftop swimming pool. Hoa designed a cozy wooden cottage in a flower valley. Everyone showcased great imagination.",
      "clozeText": "At lunchtime, most students head down to the school canteen on the ground floor. The canteen serves healthy (23) ________ such as steamed rice, grilled chicken, and fresh vegetable soup. Students must stand in a (24) ________ to buy lunch tickets. The food is cooked cleanly and tastes (25) ________. After finishing lunch, everyone carries their plastic trays to the washing (26) ________. The canteen staff are always cheerful and (27) ________.",
      "readingText": "Nestled in an ancient garden near the Perfume River, Mr. Tuan's house is an outstanding example of eco-friendly architecture.\n\nThe house is constructed mainly from renewable bamboo, recycled brick, and local timber. Solar panels installed on the roof generate all the electricity needed for lighting and domestic appliances. Rainwater is gathered in large ceramic jars, filtered naturally through charcoal and sand, and reused for watering the organic garden.\n\nLarge sliding bamboo screens allow natural ventilation to cool the house without relying on air conditioners. This thoughtful design proves that modern living can exist in complete harmony with mother nature.",
      "passageText": "📖 ĐOẠN VĂN ĐIỀN TỪ (CLOZE TEXT):\nAt lunchtime, most students head down to the school canteen on the ground floor. The canteen serves healthy (23) ________ such as steamed rice, grilled chicken, and fresh vegetable soup. Students must stand in a (24) ________ to buy lunch tickets. The food is cooked cleanly and tastes (25) ________. After finishing lunch, everyone carries their plastic trays to the washing (26) ________. The canteen staff are always cheerful and (27) ________.\n\n📖 ĐOẠN VĂN ĐỌC HIỂU (READING PASSAGE):\nNestled in an ancient garden near the Perfume River, Mr. Tuan's house is an outstanding example of eco-friendly architecture.\n\nThe house is constructed mainly from renewable bamboo, recycled brick, and local timber. Solar panels installed on the roof generate all the electricity needed for lighting and domestic appliances. Rainwater is gathered in large ceramic jars, filtered naturally through charcoal and sand, and reused for watering the organic garden.\n\nLarge sliding bamboo screens allow natural ventilation to cool the house without relying on air conditioners. This thoughtful design proves that modern living can exist in complete harmony with mother nature.",
