      "audioScript": "Alex: Hi Hoa, look at this album! My coin collection now contains over one hundred different coins.\nHoa: That is fascinating, Alex! Where do you get coins from foreign countries?\nAlex: My uncle travels internationally for business and brings coins back for me. I also swap duplicate coins with school club members.\nHoa: Which coin is your most treasured possession?\nAlex: A silver British one-pound coin minted in 2017 with a hologram image.\nHoa: Coin collecting must teach you a lot about world geography and history.\nAlex: Definitely! Each coin tells a story about national landmarks, famous leaders, and local heritage.",
      "clozeText": "Origami, the traditional Japanese art of paper folding, is my favourite indoor leisure activity. All you need is a square sheet of colourful paper and a bit of patience. You do not need any glue, scissors, or tape to create intricate animal shapes like flying cranes, hopping frogs, and blooming lotuses. Whenever I feel stressed with difficult homework, folding paper helps me calm down and focus my mind. Last week, I folded fifty miniature paper cranes to decorate my study corner. My mother loves them and thinks origami develops wonderful artistic skills.",
      "readingText": "Origami is an ancient Japanese art form that transforms simple square sheets of paper into sophisticated geometric sculptures.\n\nUnlike Western paper crafts that rely on scissors and adhesives, pure origami permits only precise folding techniques. Practitioners must follow detailed crease patterns and exercise intense spatial awareness to fold lifelike birds, dragons, and floral blossoms.\n\nPsychologists emphasize that practicing origami fosters mindfulness, improves fine motor dexterity, and strengthens spatial reasoning in teenagers. It is a tranquil bridge where mathematics meets artistic elegance.",
