      "audioScript": "David: Good morning Mai! Yesterday my brother and I went birdwatching in Ba Vi National Park.\nMai: Wow David, did you spot any rare bird species in the forest?\nMai: What equipment did you carry along the trail?\nDavid: Just a pair of 8x42 binoculars, a Vietnamese bird guidebook, and a small notebook.\nMai: Did you actually see colourful birds?\nDavid: Yes! We observed a pair of silver pheasants feeding peacefully and heard the melodious call of a laughingthrush.\nMai: Walking quietly in the woods must be a fantastic way to enjoy nature.\nDavid: It really is. It teaches you to be observant and respectful of wild creatures.",
      "clozeText": "During hot summer months, intense sunlight can cause significant damage to your delicate skin. If you spend too much time outdoors without protection, you may get severe sun burn. Doctors advise wearing a broad-brimmed hat, sunglasses, and applying sunscreen fifteen minutes before going out. In addition, drink plenty of water throughout the day to keep your body properly hydrated. Eating foods rich in vitamin C like oranges and strawberries also strengthens your immune system. Remember that taking good care of your skin is an essential part of daily personal care. Prevention is always better than cure.",
      "readingText": "Ultraviolet radiation from intense tropical sunlight poses serious risks to adolescent skin.\n\nDermatologists emphasize that unprotected sun exposure damages skin cells, accelerates premature aging, and causes agonizing sunburns. Adolescents who participate in outdoor sports must apply broad-spectrum sunscreen with an SPF of at least 30, reapplying every two hours when perspiring.\n\nWearing UV-blocking sunglasses and wide-brimmed hats provides additional physical barriers against harsh rays. Consistent sun protection is a lifelong shield that preserves dermatological health.",
      "passageText": "📖 ĐOẠN VĂN ĐIỀN TỪ (CLOZE TEXT):\nDuring hot summer months, intense sunlight can cause significant damage to your delicate skin. If you spend too much time outdoors without protection, you may get severe sun burn. Doctors advise wearing a broad-brimmed hat, sunglasses, and applying sunscreen fifteen minutes before going out. In addition, drink plenty of water throughout the day to keep your body properly hydrated. Eating foods rich in vitamin C like oranges and strawberries also strengthens your immune system. Remember that taking good care of your skin is an essential part of daily personal care. Prevention is always better than cure.\n\n📖 ĐOẠN VĂN ĐỌC HIỂU (READING PASSAGE):\nUltraviolet radiation from intense tropical sunlight poses serious risks to adolescent skin.\n\nDermatologists emphasize that unprotected sun exposure damages skin cells, accelerates premature aging, and causes agonizing sunburns. Adolescents who participate in outdoor sports must apply broad-spectrum sunscreen with an SPF of at least 30, reapplying every two hours when perspiring.\n\nWearing UV-blocking sunglasses and wide-brimmed hats provides additional physical barriers against harsh rays. Consistent sun protection is a lifelong shield that preserves dermatological health.",
      "writingRubric": "Tiêu chí chấm điểm (1.0đ): How hobbies reduce academic stress; How they develop patience and creativity; Why spending time away from screens is good.",
      "sampleWritingText": "In my opinion, engaging in productive activities during free time plays a crucial role in teenagers' overall development. First, it provides a healthy balance between rigorous academic studies and physical recreation, helping students alleviate stress. Furthermore, participating in school clubs or community projects broadens practical knowledge and strengthens social bonding. Therefore, choosing meaningful hobbies is essential for a joyful and well-rounded teenage life.",
