      "audioScript": "Ben: Hi Trang, have you ever tried equestrian sports or horseback riding?\nTrang: No Ben, horses look rather majestic but intimidating! Do you ride horses regularly?\nBen: My parents enrolled me in a riding course at the suburban sports club every Sunday morning.\nTrang: What safety protective gear must riders wear?\nBen: You must always wear a certified safety helmet, riding boots with low heels, and protective gloves.\nTrang: Is it difficult to communicate with a horse?\nBen: Horses sense your emotions quickly. If you remain calm and use gentle rein cues, the horse responds cooperatively.\nTrang: It sounds like an exhilarating hobby that builds great posture and confidence!",
      "clozeText": "Making pottery is a traditional Vietnamese craft that has fascinated me since childhood. During a weekend workshop in Bat Trang ceramic village, an experienced artisan taught me how to shape wet clay on a rotating potter's wheel. It requires steady hands and delicate touch to mold a smooth vase or a tea cup. After shaping the clay, the pottery is dried in the sun, painted with intricate floral patterns, and baked in a high-temperature kiln. Holding a finished handmade ceramic mug created by my own hands was an unforgettable feeling. This creative hobby has taught me to appreciate traditional cultural heritage.",
      "readingText": "Bat Trang ceramic village, located along the Red River near Hanoi, has been producing exquisite pottery for over seven centuries.\n\nMaster artisans still preserve time-honored techniques, hand-throwing wet clay on revolving kick-wheels and formulating natural ash glazes. The process requires immense patience; a single decorative vase undergoes drying, delicate hand-painting, and rigorous firing in gas-fueled kilns exceeding 1,200 degrees Celsius.\n\nVisiting Bat Trang offers students a profound encounter with Vietnam's living craft traditions, demonstrating how generational heritage adapts creatively to modern aesthetics.",
