      "audioUrl": "/web-tieng-anh/audio/listening_7_gk1.mp3",
      "audioScript": "Mark: Look An, I am carving a small wooden bookmark shaped like an oak leaf!\nAn: That looks delicate Mark! Where did you learn wood carving skills?\nMark: My grandfather is a traditional wood craftsman in Ha Nam. He taught me how to handle small chisels safely.\nAn: What kind of timber do you usually work on?\nMark: We carve soft timbers like basswood or pine because they have even grains and don't split easily.\nAn: Isn't handling sharp chisels hazardous for teens?\nMark: You must wear cut-resistant gloves and always carve away from your body. Safety rules are paramount in wood crafting.\nAn: Creating lasting functional art from wood is truly admirable!",
      "clozeText": "In modern digital education, secondary students spend significant time studying with laptops, tablets, and smartphones. However, prolonged screen exposure often leads to eye fatigue and blurred vision. Eye doctors recommend following the 20-20-20 rule: every twenty minutes, look away from your screen at an object twenty feet away for at least twenty seconds. Furthermore, ensure that your study room has adequate ambient lighting so that the screen does not contrast harshly with the darkness. Blinking frequently and doing simple eye exercises also help keep your eyes moist and healthy. Taking proactive care of your vision ensures long-term academic success.",
      "readingText": "Computer Vision Syndrome has emerged as a pervasive health issue among modern adolescents attending online classes.\n\nStaring at digital monitors for prolonged intervals reduces natural blinking frequency from sixteen times per minute to fewer than six, resulting in dry, stinging eyes and chronic headaches. Blue light emitted by screens also disrupts melatonin secretion, impairing sleep quality.\n\nOptometrists urge students to position screens slightly below eye level, adjust display brightness to match room illumination, and strictly follow the 20-20-20 respite rule to safeguard ocular health.",
      "passageText": "📖 ĐOẠN VĂN ĐIỀN TỪ (CLOZE TEXT):\nIn modern digital education, secondary students spend significant time studying with laptops, tablets, and smartphones. However, prolonged screen exposure often leads to eye fatigue and blurred vision. Eye doctors recommend following the 20-20-20 rule: every twenty minutes, look away from your screen at an object twenty feet away for at least twenty seconds. Furthermore, ensure that your study room has adequate ambient lighting so that the screen does not contrast harshly with the darkness. Blinking frequently and doing simple eye exercises also help keep your eyes moist and healthy. Taking proactive care of your vision ensures long-term academic success.\n\n📖 ĐOẠN VĂN ĐỌC HIỂU (READING PASSAGE):\nComputer Vision Syndrome has emerged as a pervasive health issue among modern adolescents attending online classes.\n\nStaring at digital monitors for prolonged intervals reduces natural blinking frequency from sixteen times per minute to fewer than six, resulting in dry, stinging eyes and chronic headaches. Blue light emitted by screens also disrupts melatonin secretion, impairing sleep quality.\n\nOptometrists urge students to position screens slightly below eye level, adjust display brightness to match room illumination, and strictly follow the 20-20-20 respite rule to safeguard ocular health.",
      "writingRubric": "Tiêu chí chấm điểm (1.0đ): Where and when you went; The artisan who guided you; Steps to shape wet clay; How you felt holding the final ceramic piece.",
      "sampleWritingText": "In my opinion, engaging in productive activities during free time plays a crucial role in teenagers' overall development. First, it provides a healthy balance between rigorous academic studies and physical recreation, helping students alleviate stress. Furthermore, participating in school clubs or community projects broadens practical knowledge and strengthens social bonding. Therefore, choosing meaningful hobbies is essential for a joyful and well-rounded teenage life.",
