      "audioScript": "Minh: Alex, I noticed you chose a salad with grilled chicken and brown rice for lunch!\nAlex: Yes Minh, I am trying to follow a balanced diet to boost my energy for sports.\nMinh: My doctor said fast food and deep-fried snacks contain too much unhealthy fat and sodium.\nAlex: That is true. Consuming too many potato chips and burgers makes you feel sluggish and can cause skin acne.\nMinh: What foods should secondary school students prioritize?\nAlex: Bright green vegetables like spinach, fresh seasonal fruits, lean meat, fish, and whole grains.\nMinh: What about sweet desserts and carbonated drinks?\nAlex: They spike your blood sugar temporarily, but lead to rapid fatigue later. Fresh fruit is a far healthier snack!",
      "clozeText": "Wood carving has been an esteemed craft passed down through four generations in my family in Bac Ninh. My uncle uses specialized steel chisels and fine gouges to carve exquisite lotus flowers into mahogany panels. The natural fragrance of cedar and teak wood fills the workshop. Observing flat timber transform into sculptural relief teaches me the virtue of perseverance. I spend my Sunday afternoons learning basic chisel strokes under my uncle's watchful guidance. This traditional hobby connects me deeply with my family roots.",
      "readingText": "Wood carving represents one of Vietnam's most sophisticated architectural and sculptural traditions.\n\nIn traditional carving villages, artisans transform raw blocks of jackfruit, mahogany, and rosewood into elaborate dragon bas-reliefs, temple altar panels, and delicate figurines. Every finished piece requires weeks of meticulous carving, sanding with wet sandpaper, and polishing with natural beeswax.\n\nPreserving wood carving allows younger generations to appreciate the immense skill required to craft timeless beauty entirely by human hand.",
      "passageText": "📖 ĐOẠN VĂN ĐIỀN TỪ (CLOZE TEXT):\nWood carving has been an esteemed craft passed down through four generations in my family in Bac Ninh. My uncle uses specialized steel chisels and fine gouges to carve exquisite lotus flowers into mahogany panels. The natural fragrance of cedar and teak wood fills the workshop. Observing flat timber transform into sculptural relief teaches me the virtue of perseverance. I spend my Sunday afternoons learning basic chisel strokes under my uncle's watchful guidance. This traditional hobby connects me deeply with my family roots.\n\n📖 ĐOẠN VĂN ĐỌC HIỂU (READING PASSAGE):\nWood carving represents one of Vietnam's most sophisticated architectural and sculptural traditions.\n\nIn traditional carving villages, artisans transform raw blocks of jackfruit, mahogany, and rosewood into elaborate dragon bas-reliefs, temple altar panels, and delicate figurines. Every finished piece requires weeks of meticulous carving, sanding with wet sandpaper, and polishing with natural beeswax.\n\nPreserving wood carving allows younger generations to appreciate the immense skill required to craft timeless beauty entirely by human hand.",
