      "audioScript": "Mai: Good morning Peter! You look very cheerful and energetic today.\nPeter: Good morning Mai! That is because I went to bed at nine thirty and got nine hours of peaceful sleep.\nMai: That is wonderful. Many of our classmates stay up past midnight scrolling on smartphones.\nPeter: Late screen time emits blue light which disrupts the body's natural sleep cycle.\nMai: How does getting eight to nine hours of sleep benefit you at school?\nPeter: My concentration during morning lectures is much sharper, and I remember vocabulary words easily.\nMai: Sleeping well also strengthens our immune system against common colds, doesn't it?\nPeter: Absolutely! Quality sleep is the cornerstone of good health for teenagers.",
      "clozeText": "Last month, our school woodworking club partnered with local carpenters to construct wooden bookshelves for rural schools. We measured pine planks, smoothed rough edges with electric sanders, and assembled sturdy four-tier bookcases. After coating the wood with eco-friendly protective lacquer, we stocked the shelves with hundreds of illustrated science and literature books. Transporting these bookcases to highland classrooms brought tears of joy to local teachers. Creating functional educational furniture through teamwork was deeply satisfying.",
      "readingText": "Access to physical reading materials remains severely restricted in many impoverished remote hamlets across Vietnam.\n\nRecognizing that books require proper storage to withstand humid mountain climates, our community club initiated the 'Shelves of Hope' timber project. Students spent three consecutive weekends cutting, sanding, and assembling sturdy pine library units capable of housing hundreds of volumes.\n\nThe finished bookcases were delivered alongside thousands of donated storybooks, establishing vibrant reading corners that ignite children's imaginations.",
      "passageText": "📖 ĐOẠN VĂN ĐIỀN TỪ (CLOZE TEXT):\nLast month, our school woodworking club partnered with local carpenters to construct wooden bookshelves for rural schools. We measured pine planks, smoothed rough edges with electric sanders, and assembled sturdy four-tier bookcases. After coating the wood with eco-friendly protective lacquer, we stocked the shelves with hundreds of illustrated science and literature books. Transporting these bookcases to highland classrooms brought tears of joy to local teachers. Creating functional educational furniture through teamwork was deeply satisfying.\n\n📖 ĐOẠN VĂN ĐỌC HIỂU (READING PASSAGE):\nAccess to physical reading materials remains severely restricted in many impoverished remote hamlets across Vietnam.\n\nRecognizing that books require proper storage to withstand humid mountain climates, our community club initiated the 'Shelves of Hope' timber project. Students spent three consecutive weekends cutting, sanding, and assembling sturdy pine library units capable of housing hundreds of volumes.\n\nThe finished bookcases were delivered alongside thousands of donated storybooks, establishing vibrant reading corners that ignite children's imaginations.",
