      "audioScript": "Minh: Mai, look at all these cardboard boxes in the youth union office!\nMai: Hi Minh! These are warm clothes and blankets donated by students for the 'Warm Winter' campaign.\nMinh: Where will these donations be delivered?\nMai: To primary and secondary pupils living in remote highland villages in Ha Giang.\nMinh: Highland winters are bitterly cold and windy, aren't they?\nMai: Yes, temperatures often drop below five degrees Celsius, and many children lack adequate winter coats.\nMinh: Our class collected fifty jackets, thirty woolen hats, and forty pairs of socks.\nMai: Let's pack them carefully into weatherproof bags so they arrive in perfect condition.",
      "clozeText": "During dry winter weather, low humidity and biting winds frequently cause lips to become painfully chapped and cracked. Because lips lack sebaceous oil glands, they lose moisture much faster than facial skin. Dermatologists emphasize that licking lips worsens chapping because evaporating saliva dehydrates tissue further. The best remedy is applying natural beeswax or petroleum lip balm several times daily. Drinking sufficient water and running a cool-mist humidifier in your bedroom also preserves skin hydration effectively.",
      "readingText": "Cheilitis, commonly known as chapped lips, is an uncomfortable dermatological condition triggered by low ambient humidity and harsh winds.\n\nUnlike surrounding facial skin, lips possess an exceptionally thin stratum corneum and lack protective oil-secreting glands, leaving them acutely vulnerable to moisture depletion. Continually licking dry lips creates an irritant cycle where digestive enzymes in saliva erode lip mucosa.\n\nDermatologists recommend thick occlusive ointments containing ceramides, shea butter, or pure petroleum jelly to establish a resilient protective seal against winter air.",
